## Getting started

This repo is built by an issue-driven agent factory. Only the maintainer (@[[ maintainer_github ]]) can start or advance work, merge to `main`, or publish anything. The quickest way in is the **`factory` skill** (`.claude/skills/factory/`), which runs the whole pipeline from a GitHub Copilot or Claude Code session:

1. Run `./scripts/bootstrap.sh` once, if you haven't (it creates the GitHub repo, labels and board; EMU accounts use `--private`). The skill needs no secret.
2. Start GitHub Copilot (`copilot`, or the Copilot app) or Claude Code (`claude`) in this repo, on the orchestrator model from [`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md) → "Models" (`/model` checks it).
3. Say **"brainstorm X into issues"** or **"run the factory"**.[% if actions_mode %] It never adds `factory:design` or `design:approved`, so the Actions workflows don't duplicate the work.[% endif %]

The skill's scripts need Python 3.9 or newer and run as `python3`. Step-by-step: [first run](https://rahmansunny071.github.io/amplifactory/getting-started/first-run/); everything else is in the [docs site](https://rahmansunny071.github.io/amplifactory/). Agent rules live in [`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md); the stage prompts in [`.claude/commands/`](.claude/commands/).

### What the skill does

- **Brainstorm → lanes → issues:** talk an idea through with the orchestrator (your session). It proposes **lanes** (features, with priorities `P0`–`P3` and dependencies between lanes) and the **ordered issues** in each, you confirm once, and they're created as lane issues with native sub-issues, "blocked by" links and priority labels (and added to the Project board).
- **Lanes:** each lane's sub-issues run one at a time, in order; lanes run **one at a time by default**, and the orchestrator asks before running more in parallel (`queue.py` shows each lane's current issue and what to work on now).
- **Run:** per issue: **one builder** plans, builds and fixes, and **one fresh reviewer** reviews, in parallel with CI. Critical issues (privacy/security, new dependencies, shared data contracts) also get a design review. The orchestrator merges when the gates in `references/gates.md` pass. By default the orchestrator and reviewers run on Claude Opus and the builder on Claude Sonnet; `CLAUDE.md` → "Models" sets them for Claude Code and for Copilot.
- **Urgency first:** in the `mvp` release phase (`CLAUDE.md`), only blockers are fixed in-PR; everything else is filed as an `improvement` issue.
- **Flags and continues:** when it needs you (it's unsure, a gate fails, or it would rely on an unverified assumption about real data), it asks **inline on the PR** with options and a recommendation, labels it `factory:needs-maintainer`, and works on other issues. Reply in the threads, then say "resume the factory".
- **Real data:** whether agents may read your real data is set in `CLAUDE.md` ("Real data": `never`, `with-approval` or `allowed`). At every level, real data is never committed or quoted in GitHub text, and tests use synthetic fixtures.
- **Docs ship with the code:** every PR updates the docs its change affects, and its Handoff says which (or why none). Docs that disagree with the PR's code block the merge; older mismatches become sub-issues of the "Documentation tech-debt" issue, fixed when a later PR touches that file.
- **Ready to merge:** if every gate passes but the merge is refused (the harness denies it or GitHub rejects it), the skill never works around it. It labels the PR `factory:ready-to-merge`, comments the merge command and carries on; `queue.py` and the end-of-run report list what waits for you.
- **Handoffs:** every PR keeps a "## Handoff" section, so a new session or reviewer starts from it. Its gist is saved to `.factory/designs/` at merge.
- **Run log:** `.factory/runs/` records each step, so a fresh session resumes mid-issue; `scripts/runlog.py summary` reports time and tokens per issue.

**Reporting a factory problem.** If the factory itself got in the way (not this project's code), say "file a factory lesson: ...". The **`factory-lesson` skill** (`.claude/skills/factory-lesson/`) finds the template repo from `.copier-answers.yml`, checks for a duplicate `lesson` issue, drafts the issue without real data values, secrets or private paths, and files it there after one confirmation from you. It can then link the lesson on the PR or issue where you hit it, if you agree. It needs a `gh` login with access to the template repo.

**Letting the factory merge for you (opt-in).** By default, when every gate passes and the merge is refused, the skill labels the PR `factory:ready-to-merge`, comments the exact merge command and moves on; you merge them in one batch. If you would rather it merged itself, you can add a Claude Code permission rule yourself, in `.claude/settings.json` or your user `~/.claude/settings.json`:

```json
{ "permissions": { "allow": ["Bash(gh pr merge * --merge --admin --delete-branch)"] } }
```

This uses the wildcard syntax from the [Claude Code permissions docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions). The rule lets the agent use your ruleset bypass (`--admin`), so it is your choice and the template does not ship it. The rule matches only commands that end in `--delete-branch`, which is how the skill runs the merge (`references/gates.md` → "Merge"); variants with `-R` or other trailing flags aren't covered. A project rule applies only after you accept the workspace trust prompt. How allow rules interact with auto mode is described in the [permission modes docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes); check it, and try it in your own session. Even with the rule, a merge GitHub itself refuses is still handed off.

**In GitHub Copilot** the rule above does nothing (Copilot doesn't read `permissions` from `.claude/settings.json`), and Copilot's own rules can't single out the merge: `--allow-tool='shell(gh pr)'`, or "Yes, and don't ask again" on a `gh pr merge` prompt, approves every `gh pr` command, `gh pr close` included. Instead, add a personal hook that runs the skill's `merge_hook.py`. It approves only `gh pr merge <N> --merge [--admin] --delete-branch`, typed exactly so, and leaves every other request to the normal prompt. Copy the script out of the repo first, so no branch or file edit can change what it approves (copy it again after a factory update):

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.copilot/hooks && cp .claude/skills/factory/scripts/merge_hook.py ~/.copilot/hooks/
```

On Windows, in PowerShell:

```powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force ~/.copilot/hooks | Out-Null; Copy-Item .claude/skills/factory/scripts/merge_hook.py ~/.copilot/hooks/
```

Then save this as `~/.copilot/hooks/factory-merge.json`. If you set `COPILOT_HOME`, Copilot reads personal hooks from `$COPILOT_HOME/hooks/` instead, so put both files there:

```json
{ "version": 1, "hooks": { "permissionRequest": [ { "type": "command", "matcher": "bash|powershell", "timeoutSec": 10,
  "bash": "test -f \"$COPILOT_HOOK_DIR/merge_hook.py\" && python3 \"$COPILOT_HOOK_DIR/merge_hook.py\" || true",
  "powershell": "$f = Join-Path $env:COPILOT_HOOK_DIR 'merge_hook.py'; if (Test-Path $f) { [Console]::In.ReadToEnd() | python $f }; exit 0" } ] } }
```

Copilot runs the `bash` command on macOS and Linux and the `powershell` one on Windows, where it calls Python as
`python` (use `py` if that's how Python runs there). It applies in every repo you use Copilot in, for that one command; a missing script or any error falls back to the normal prompt. Don't commit it as a repository hook (`.github/hooks/`): that would run the working tree's copy of the script, which any file edit could turn into an allow-all. The details are in the [Copilot hooks reference](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/hooks-reference). As with the Claude Code rule, the template doesn't ship it, and a merge that is still refused is handed off.

### Labels

Bootstrap creates these. Meanings in short; the [labels reference](https://rahmansunny071.github.io/amplifactory/reference/labels/) has the detail.

| Group | Label | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Skill | `factory:needs-maintainer` | The skill asked you a question on the PR; reply, then say "resume the factory" |
| Skill | `factory:ready-to-merge` | Every gate passed; you merge it (the default hand-off) |
| Skill | `improvement` | Non-blocking follow-up, filed instead of fixed in the PR |
| Skill | `documentation` | A docs todo, including the "Documentation tech-debt" sub-issues |
| Progress[% if actions_mode %] (skill and Actions)[% endif %] | `factory:designing` | Design / plan in progress or awaiting review |
| Progress[% if actions_mode %] (skill and Actions)[% endif %] | `factory:building` | Implementation in progress |
| Progress[% if actions_mode %] (skill and Actions)[% endif %] | `factory:review` | PR open, in review or awaiting merge |
| Progress[% if actions_mode %] (skill and Actions)[% endif %] | `factory:blocked` | An agent hit something it shouldn't decide, or couldn't finish |
| Actions[% if not actions_mode %] (off)[% endif %] | `factory:design` | [% if actions_mode %]You ask the design agent for a design (on an issue)[% else %]Unused: the Actions mode is off in this repo[% endif %] |
| Actions[% if not actions_mode %] (off)[% endif %] | `design:approved` | [% if actions_mode %]You sign off the design PR: start building[% else %]Unused: the Actions mode is off in this repo[% endif %] |
| Lanes | `lane` | Parent issue of a feature; its sub-issues run in order |
| Priority | `P0` | Most urgent; ranks lanes and issues |
| Priority | `P1`, `P2` | In between |
| Priority | `P3` | Least urgent |

