## Actions mode with Claude (optional)

The same pipeline can run from labels on GitHub Actions, with a click from you at every step. Its agents are Claude Code GitHub Actions, so it needs a Claude subscription: run `/install-github-app` in Claude Code, pick this repo, and it saves the `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` secret.

1. Open an issue with the **Factory task** template.
2. Add `factory:design`. The design agent writes `.factory/designs/issue-N.md` and opens a draft design PR.
3. Review it; iterate with `@claude …` comments or edit it yourself.
4. Add `design:approved` to the design PR. The build agent implements it on `factory/issue-N`, runs the checks until green and opens a PR.
5. The review agent checks the PR against the design and `CLAUDE.md`.
6. Merge. Releases stay manual.
[%- elif copilot_actions -%]
## Actions mode with Copilot (optional)

The same pipeline can run from labels on GitHub Actions, with a click from you at every step. Its agents are the GitHub Copilot CLI, signed in with each workflow's own token (`copilot-requests: write`): there is no secret and no Claude subscription. Their requests bill your Copilot seat, on the model in `.copier-answers.yml` → `copilot_model`. With `FACTORY_RUNNER=local` (see [CI](#ci)) and a private repo, they run on your Mac's runner pool, like CI.

1. Open an issue with the **Factory task** template.
2. Add `factory:design`. The design agent writes `.factory/designs/issue-N.md` and opens a draft design PR.
3. Review it, and push any changes to its `design/issue-N` branch yourself.
4. Add `design:approved` to the design PR. The build agent implements it on `factory/issue-N`, runs the checks until green and opens a PR. GitHub holds the CI and review runs of a PR the workflow token opens until someone approves them; the build workflow approves them for you.
5. The review agent checks the PR against the design and `CLAUDE.md`.
6. Merge. Releases stay manual.

The agents open their PRs with the workflow token, which needs **Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests** (Settings → Actions → General → Workflow permissions). Bootstrap turns it on; if you added this mode with `copier update`, turn it on yourself: `gh api -X PUT repos/[[ maintainer_github ]]/[[ project_name ]]/actions/permissions/workflow -F can_approve_pull_request_reviews=true`. Each agent may do only what Claude's may at the same stage: a hook, `.github/factory/tool_rules.py`, which every agent job copies from the default branch, denies the rest (other commands, `$`, redirections, and writes outside `.factory/designs/` for the design agent or at all for the review agent). Change it only in a reviewed PR. No agent may merge, and the review agent only comments. The token could still approve a PR it didn't open, so `.github/CODEOWNERS` makes you the owner of every file: with the "Protect main" ruleset, which requires a code owner's review, only your approval (or your `--admin` bypass) merges a PR. `approve-runs` approves the held runs of the build PR's branch and commit, until GitHub stops adding them, and only when the build PR, into the default branch, is the one open PR from that branch. The agents act on text in issues and comments, and on the runner pool their shell commands run as you and could read your files. So their jobs use the pool only while this repo is private (an internal repo doesn't count), and you should keep yourself the only one who can write that text.
[%- else -%]
## Actions mode (off)

This repo was generated with Copier's `actions_agent: none`, so it has no agent workflows: you run the factory with the skill. To add the label-driven Actions mode, run `uvx copier update --data actions_agent=copilot` (agents on the GitHub Copilot CLI) or `--data actions_agent=claude` (Claude Code GitHub Actions) in a PR of its own; this section then explains it.
[%- endif %]

## Changing the factory itself

[% if claude_actions -%]
- **Workflow changes go in their own PR.** `claude-code-action` refuses to run from a workflow file that differs from `main`'s, so on a PR that edits `.github/workflows/factory-*.yml` the affected agent stage skips itself (the job still shows success). The change takes effect once merged. Don't bundle it with feature work.
[% else -%]
- **Workflow changes go in their own PR** (`CLAUDE.md` → "Hard rules").[% if copilot_actions %] The Copilot agents can't make them: the workflow token can't push changes to `.github/workflows/`.[% endif %]
[% endif -%]
[% if actions_mode -%]
- **After merging a workflow change, update open factory PRs before re-triggering them.** PR-triggered runs use the PR's merge commit, which GitHub doesn't rebuild when `main` moves, so an older design or implementation PR keeps running the old workflow. Click **Update branch** on the PR (or `gh api -X PUT repos/[[ maintainer_github ]]/[[ project_name ]]/pulls/N/update-branch`), then re-add the label or push.
[% endif -%]
- **Merging your own PRs:** GitHub never lets an author approve their own PR. Use the ruleset's admin bypass ("Merge without waiting for requirements to be met").
[% if claude_actions -%]
- **A run that ends without its artifact is a failure,** even if the job is green: a design run must open a draft PR, a build run an implementation PR, otherwise the agent must leave a comment and `factory:blocked`.
[% elif copilot_actions -%]
- **A design or build run that ends without its PR turns red,** unless the agent marked the issue `factory:blocked` and commented why.
[% endif %]
## CI

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs the tests (one job per Python version) and lint on pull requests and on pushes to `main`. Two repository variables steer it, with no workflow edit:

- **`FACTORY_RUNNER`**: `local` runs the jobs on your Mac, in its runner pool (`.claude/skills/factory-ci/`). Unset or anything else: GitHub's `ubuntu-latest`. Pull requests from forks always use `ubuntu-latest`.
- **`FACTORY_CI`**: `off` skips every job. A skipped job counts as passing, so pull requests can still merge; gate on the checks in `CLAUDE.md` instead.

The pool is opt-in, for accounts whose GitHub-hosted runners are off: tell the agent "set up CI runners", and its setup ends by setting `FACTORY_RUNNER=local`. `gh variable set FACTORY_RUNNER --body github` keeps GitHub's runners, like unset. Draft pull requests skip CI; marking one ready for review runs it. Tests stop after 20 minutes and lint after 10.

